    "result_text": "Full build is green. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change assigns a `customerCode` (`<LAST3>-<NNNN>`) when creating an owner via `POST /api/owners`, persisted so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns it. Kept structural impact low by adding a small cohesive step rather than growing existing functions:\n\n1. **New function `AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 a single-method class (WMC_other = 0) that computes the code: upper-cased first \u22643 letters of `lastName`, plus a global sequence = `ownerRepository.findAll().size() + 1`, zero-padded to 4 digits.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted an `assignCode` step between `build` and `save` (under the same transaction governance), so the sequence is one more than the count of existing owners at creation time.\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added the `customerCode` field with getter/setter (no existing method touched).\n\n4. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `customer_code` column (hsqldb is the test profile).\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `customerCode` as a read-only property on the `Owner` response schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it and MapStruct maps it by name automatically.\n\nThe test asserts consecutive codes (`SMI-` then `JON-`, differing by 1), which holds because each owner's sequence is the pre-save owner count + 1.\n\nNote: I only added the column to the hsqldb schema (the profile the tests use). If you plan to run the app on the h2/mysql/postgres profiles, their `schema.sql` files would need the same `customer_code` column \u2014 say the word and I'll add them.",
